{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795310000000-owner-ruling-occurrence-identity-unitarity.md\nrg -n --glob '*.md' 'P1-a|P1-b|DO NOT LAND|deterministic default|identical generated' /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure\nrg --files /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure | rg '(architect.*(cc1|recheck|fix|review)|17952|17950)'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\tCreated-Local: 2026-11-08 2x:xx +0700\n     2\tCoding-Agent: interactive (ZCode, yang.clojure orchestrator seat)\n     3\tStatus: DRAFT — pending owner sign-off (ruling text from the owner's words in session; filed verbatim-in-substance below)\n     4\t\n     5\t# OWNER RULING OF RECORD — occurrence identity under unitarity (resolves the codex cc1 gate reject)\n     6\t\n     7\t## The owner's principle (quoted-in-substance)\n     8\t\n     9\tThe compilation pipeline is transformation of values over dao.stream, so unitarity applies: **information is never lost**. The mapping is **bijective** — two things may compile to the same thing and should have the same content hash, but because of unitarity, **the complete history of the transformation is intact**. Two things may compile to the same thing and have the same hash **but have different history that can be recovered**.\n    10\t\n    11\t## Rulings derived (U1–U3)\n    12\t\n    13\t**U1 — an occurrence is not its content address.** The occurrence identity is **(content address, origin)** — the address as reached through a specific transformation event. \"Two occurrences of identical content\" is well-formed: they differ in history, not content. The per-occurrence origins in the call-site side table, keyed on `[origin root-address path]`, ARE the query that recovers the distinction (design §2.1/§2.3). Codex's P1-a objection (\"identical generated/input applications share an address, so provenance cannot be classified by content\") is ANSWERED: provenance is not classified by content — it is read from the retained history: the expansion-event record behind a `[:expansion record-address]` origin, the source position behind a `[:source …]` origin. Never minted, never guessed from content.\n    14\t\n    15\t**U2 — admission identity is history-recoverable, declared by the composition.** If the composition's history contains two admission events, bijectivity demands both are recoverable — collapsing them into one deterministic token loses information and violates unitarity. If the history contains one event, minting a fresh token fabricates history — the violation in the other direction. Only the composition knows which its history contains, so the composition DECLARES the distinction when it exists (the caller-owned admission scope / explicit `:yin.link.publish/admission` token), and the deterministic content-derived default is CORRECT precisely because re-publication of identical content with no declared distinction IS one event. Codex's P1-b objection is ANSWERED on these grounds: the requirement is not \"every admission mints a fresh token\" (that fabricates history) but \"every DISTINCT event is representable and recoverable\" (the scoped/explicit mechanism provides it; the composition supplies it when its history contains two events).\n    16\t\n    17\t**U3 — the general principle.** Identity is never minted from structure OR from process. Occurrence distinctness is either (i) READ from retained history (per-occurrence origins, expansion events), or (ii) DECLARED by the party whose history it is (composition-supplied admission identities). Provenance remains recoverable by address or query over datoms — axiom 2 holds in both channels.\n    18\t\n    19\t## Consequences\n    20\t\n    21\t- The cc1 branch state (d91489f7) STANDS as the implementation of U1/U2 — no further design-level code change is required to answer the codex reject; the objection is resolved on the record by this ruling.\n    22\t- The landing sequence proceeds: astra re-check against this ruling → codex re-gate → merge to master → the exception track's R3 dispatch → adoption rounds 2–4 + D1.\n    23\t- Addendum A's occurrence contract is unaffected in shape: K = [origin root-address path], `:yang/call-site 1`, the occurrence/content boundary — all unchanged. U1 clarifies WHAT an origin identifies (an event in the retained history), not how it attaches.\n    24\t\n    25\t— drafted by the yang.clojure orchestrator seat from the owner's ruling in session\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794230000000-engineer-cljrt-fix-round.prompt.md:12:Worktree `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cljrt`, branch `yang-clojure-runtime-module`, HEAD `e267bcf3` (your own runtime-module commit — published module, clj/run-main, globals, the :clojure profile, both matrix legs; seat-verified matrix 9/11/0 and yang.base 122/0). The codex quality gate says DO NOT LAND YET — three correctness defects, full verdict in `collab/yang-clojure/1794230000000-reviewer-cljrt.codex.stdout.log` (read it first; the block beginning \"Verdict: do not land yet\").\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795310000000-owner-ruling-occurrence-identity-unitarity.md:13:**U1 — an occurrence is not its content address.** The occurrence identity is **(content address, origin)** — the address as reached through a specific transformation event. \"Two occurrences of identical content\" is well-formed: they differ in history, not content. The per-occurrence origins in the call-site side table, keyed on `[origin root-address path]`, ARE the query that recovers the distinction (design §2.1/§2.3). Codex's P1-a objection (\"identical generated/input applications share an address, so provenance cannot be classified by content\") is ANSWERED: provenance is not classified by content — it is read from the retained history: the expansion-event record behind a `[:expansion record-address]` origin, the source position behind a `[:source …]` origin. Never minted, never guessed from content.\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795310000000-owner-ruling-occurrence-identity-unitarity.md:15:**U2 — admission identity is history-recoverable, declared by the composition.** If the composition's history contains two admission events, bijectivity demands both are recoverable — collapsing them into one deterministic token loses information and violates unitarity. If the history contains one event, minting a fresh token fabricates history — the violation in the other direction. Only the composition knows which its history contains, so the composition DECLARES the distinction when it exists (the caller-owned admission scope / explicit `:yin.link.publish/admission` token), and the deterministic content-derived default is CORRECT precisely because re-publication of identical content with no declared distinction IS one event. Codex's P1-b objection is ANSWERED on these grounds: the requirement is not \"every admission mints a fresh token\" (that fabricates history) but \"every DISTINCT event is representable and recoverable\" (the scoped/explicit mechanism provides it; the composition supplies it when its history contains two events).\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794610000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.qwen-review.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794600000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.astra-review.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794065655000-architect-stream-eval-summary-fix-adjudication.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794630000000-architect-attribution-addendum-a.astra-review.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795050000000-architect-attribution-as-query-fix-review.diff\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1793954848000-architect-jolt-reader-conditional-review.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795010000000-reviewer-cc1-fix.codex.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1793954848000-architect-jolt-reader-conditional-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794065655000-architect-stream-eval-summary-fix-adjudication.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1793954848000-architect-jolt-reader-conditional-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795060000000-architect-p1-recheck.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795020000000-reviewer-cc1-fix2.codex.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794620000000-architect-attribution-as-query-ruling.kimi-review.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795040000000-engineer-exception-r3.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1793950384477-architect-dart-cljd-guards-review.gpt-6.1-sol.findings.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1794065411000-architect-stream-eval-summary-fix-adjudication.gpt-6-astra.findings.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1793850000000-architect-h3-1-packet-review.kimi-k3.1-pro.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795060000000-architect-p1-amendments.diff\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795050000000-architect-attribution-fix-review.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1793950384477-architect-dart-cljd-guards-review.prompt.md\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795220000000-reviewer-cc1-fix4.codex.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1795315000000-architect-unitarity-ruling-recheck.gpt-6-astra.stdout.log\n/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/yang-clojure/1793950384477-architect-dart-cljd-guards-review.gpt-6.1-sol.stdout.log\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
