{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"agent_message","text":"NOT ENDORSED\n\nAssessed `d91489f7` against U1–U3 as requested. U2 resolves the prior admission-default objection; U1’s implementation premise remains false.\n\n- **U1 / P1-a — not satisfied.** `expand-call` builds an input **address set**, then assigns incoming provenance to any output whose address belongs to that set ([macro.cljc:1087](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1087), [macro.cljc:1140](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1140)). This still classifies provenance by content equality. Consider a macro returning an `if` containing both an admitted `(f 1)` operand and a freshly constructed identical `(f 1)`. Both receive the source origin; the constructed application should name the expansion event. Their different final paths distinguish positions, but do not correctly identify their different histories.\n\n  Recording that result by path does not repair the classification ([macro.cljc:1200](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1200)). Expansion records retain attempt, parent, input root, call path, macro root and output root, but no per-output-occurrence retention/generation correspondence ([macro.cljc:1005](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1005)). U1 requires history-derived origins; it does not authorize this address-membership inference.\n\n- **U2 / P1-b — satisfied for C1 publication.** Explicit admission tokens take precedence, scoped calls allocate distinct ordinals, and undeclared identical publication uses the deterministic default ([publish.cljc:277](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:277)). Under U2, that default is correct. The previous keyword-namespace/type collision is fixed by `pr-str` ([publish.cljc:136](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:136)). Origins are persisted and retrieved from the record ([publish.cljc:327](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:327), [publish.cljc:450](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/linker/publish.cljc:450)). Distinct events are representable, with scope uniqueness and replay identity owned by the composition.\n\n- **Site-table identity — structurally sound, insufficient to establish provenance.** Tables are partitioned by admission origin and final root; rows additionally carry occurrence paths ([macro.cljc:1491](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1491)). This preserves correctly supplied distinctions. It cannot recover the generating origin incorrectly replaced with a source origin above.\n\n- **Multi-claim validator / P2 — closed.** Every validator mode now rejects multiple origins claiming one `[root path]` within a single coverage table, reporting both claims ([clojure.cljc:199](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:199)). This checks consistency, not historical truth.\n\n- **Additional U1/U3 gap — record retention is not guaranteed.** `macro/load` retains the returned expansion log only when a log writer exists ([macro.cljc:1522](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/vm/macro.cljc:1522)); the production REPL explicitly constructs its expander without one ([repl.cljc:732](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-cc1/src/cljc/yin/repl.cljc:732)). Consequently, retained site rows can name expansion-record addresses whose records this path does not retain. The ruling’s premise of queryable retained history does not itself supply that retention.\n\nNo files modified; no tests run."}}
